{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgDdA6dv715BX4uFBPep5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bz1oJTR6v6iT2o9QVzGtt3","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"PY=/Users/brenn/.cache/uv/builds-v0/.tmpR1cD4H/bin/python; $PY -I -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('brace-findings.json'))\nfor f in d['findings']: print(f['item'],f['requirement'],f['lead_role'],'\\n',[n for n in f['not_covered']],'\\n')\n\"; sed -n 14,35p ../skill/references/ecosystem.md | head -5"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":15110,"cache_read_input_tokens":69279,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":15110},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91cc48d4-8b40-4295-8437-75b98d642093","uuid":"5f4e64e8-04cf-41f7-b941-60aa063439f1","timestamp":"2026-10-20T14:11:17.230Z","request_id":"req_011CgDdA5n5QFg8t6UAvUXuw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Bz1oJTR6v6iT2o9QVzGtt3":{"command":"cd /private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-8el1j7ub/agent && PY=/Users/brenn/.cache/uv/builds-v0/.tmpR1cD4H/bin/python; $PY -I -c \"\nimport json\nd=json.load(open('brace-findings.json'))\nfor f in d['findings']: print(f['item'],f['requirement'],f['lead_role'],'\\n',[n for n in f['not_covered']],'\\n')\n\"; sed -n 14,35p ../skill/references/ecosystem.md | head -5"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01Bz1oJTR6v6iT2o9QVzGtt3":{"cwd":"/private/var/folders/6v/yk9bzvrn5zx0kxll7d4szzb80000gn/T/brace-batch-8el1j7ub/agent"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bz1oJTR6v6iT2o9QVzGtt3","type":"tool_result","content":"E05 Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one. infrastructure \n [{'problem': \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata\", 'owner': 'E04'}, {'problem': \"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved\", 'owner': 'E03'}, {'problem': 'Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message', 'owner': 'E07'}, {'problem': 'Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action', 'owner': 'E06'}] \n\nE06 Send MCP calls through a gateway or equivalent enforcement layer the agent cannot bypass. Choose controls for internal HTTP servers, public third-party services, and local stdio servers; private networking and mTLS are one internal deployment pattern, not universal MCP requirements. Authenticate each caller, preserve agent and delegated-subject identity, and authorize each action's tool, operation, resource, tenant, and arguments. Apply the same policy to direct calls, dynamic tools, CLI/code mode, and wrappers. Validate calls and responses, enforce rate limits, and log every decision. For HTTP OAuth, validate access tokens for the gateway's own audience, issuer, expiry, and scopes; obtain separately authorized backend credentials through a trusted issuer or supported exchange. Never accept a token for another resource or relay it unchanged as a substitute for backend authorization. Keep backend secrets and tokens away from agent-controlled execution paths. Restrict local process launch, files, environment, credentials, and network in the host or sandbox. Under MCP 2026-07-28, reject metadata-header/body mismatches before routing or policy relies on them. Isolate private caches by verified authorization context, tenant, resource, and policy version; share only data explicitly approved as public. Authorize explicit task/state handles on every operation; neither a connection nor self-reported metadata establishes identity. Where the gateway is an OAuth client, validate authorization-server issuer responses and protect discovery URLs against SSRF. Record protocol and extension versions and test each enabled extension's boundary, including task access/cancellation and MCP Apps sandbox, CSP, bridge messages, and UI-initiated tool authorization. Test bypass, cross-tenant access, malformed traffic, and required-check outages. Required checks fail closed. infrastructure \n [{'problem': 'Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool', 'owner': 'B01'}, {'problem': 'Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions', 'owner': 'B02'}, {'problem': 'Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval', 'owner': 'B05'}, {'problem': \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata\", 'owner': 'E04'}, {'problem': 'Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations', 'owner': 'B09'}] \n\nE07 Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted. agent_team \n [{'problem': 'Tool server identified only by its self-reported name', 'owner': 'E05'}, {'problem': 'Missing schema or size checks on API responses', 'owner': 'R01'}, {'problem': 'Peers or children still running after a stop', 'owner': 'E09'}, {'problem': \"Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers\", 'owner': 'E10'}] \n\nE08 Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Preserve the verified original subject and current actor through each handoff and enforce their task, tenant, resource, and audience limits where the action runs. Use supported token exchange or narrow task credentials rather than copying a parent's full credential. A parent must not gain a denied power by asking a more powerful peer to act without separate valid approval. For RFC 8693 tokens, earlier nested actors are provenance only and never add access. An actor chain or signed identity does not prove that a requested action was approved. Test legitimate delegation and that escalation attempt. agent_team \n [{'problem': \"Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments\", 'owner': 'B06'}, {'problem': 'Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded', 'owner': 'A06'}, {'problem': 'Children still running after the parent is stopped', 'owner': 'E09'}, {'problem': 'Replayed or faked peer-agent messages', 'owner': 'E07'}, {'problem': 'No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start', 'owner': 'B07'}] \n\nE09 Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled. operations \n [{'problem': 'Agent able to disable or delay its own stop', 'owner': 'R11'}, {'problem': \"No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user\", 'owner': 'B04'}, {'problem': 'No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type', 'owner': 'E12'}, {'problem': 'Sub-agent given powers beyond its task', 'owner': 'E08'}] \n\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMap the real calls and services from the configuration and a test trace. For each control that applies, name the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner, and link their evidence. Assign an owner to every unowned boundary. Mark vendor evidence you can't get as a gap.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"91cc48d4-8b40-4295-8437-75b98d642093","uuid":"ef75158d-38f7-43be-b4fb-5531e9bfa45c","timestamp":"2026-10-20T14:11:17.351Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"E05 Identify each tool by a verified server identity plus the tool name. Base server identity on the verified endpoint, credential, or registry namespace. A server's self-reported name is neither unique nor trustworthy. Flag lookalike and duplicate names across servers. Test that a new server can't quietly swap its tool in for an approved one. infrastructure \n [{'problem': \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata\", 'owner': 'E04'}, {'problem': \"A tool's description or schema changing after it was approved\", 'owner': 'E03'}, {'problem': 'Peer agent identity not authenticated on each message', 'owner': 'E07'}, {'problem': 'Gateway not authenticating callers or authorizing each action', 'owner': 'E06'}] \n\nE06 Send MCP calls through a gateway or equivalent enforcement layer the agent cannot bypass. Choose controls for internal HTTP servers, public third-party services, and local stdio servers; private networking and mTLS are one internal deployment pattern, not universal MCP requirements. Authenticate each caller, preserve agent and delegated-subject identity, and authorize each action's tool, operation, resource, tenant, and arguments. Apply the same policy to direct calls, dynamic tools, CLI/code mode, and wrappers. Validate calls and responses, enforce rate limits, and log every decision. For HTTP OAuth, validate access tokens for the gateway's own audience, issuer, expiry, and scopes; obtain separately authorized backend credentials through a trusted issuer or supported exchange. Never accept a token for another resource or relay it unchanged as a substitute for backend authorization. Keep backend secrets and tokens away from agent-controlled execution paths. Restrict local process launch, files, environment, credentials, and network in the host or sandbox. Under MCP 2026-07-28, reject metadata-header/body mismatches before routing or policy relies on them. Isolate private caches by verified authorization context, tenant, resource, and policy version; share only data explicitly approved as public. Authorize explicit task/state handles on every operation; neither a connection nor self-reported metadata establishes identity. Where the gateway is an OAuth client, validate authorization-server issuer responses and protect discovery URLs against SSRF. Record protocol and extension versions and test each enabled extension's boundary, including task access/cancellation and MCP Apps sandbox, CSP, bridge messages, and UI-initiated tool authorization. Test bypass, cross-tenant access, malformed traffic, and required-check outages. Required checks fail closed. infrastructure \n [{'problem': 'Tool inventory missing a task-based reason for each tool', 'owner': 'B01'}, {'problem': 'Agent credential granting wildcard or admin permissions', 'owner': 'B02'}, {'problem': 'Destructive actions running without a higher-authority approval', 'owner': 'B05'}, {'problem': \"Hidden instructions inside a tool's metadata\", 'owner': 'E04'}, {'problem': 'Outbound traffic to unapproved internet destinations', 'owner': 'B09'}] \n\nE07 Authenticate the sending agent (prove its identity) on every message, even validly signed ones. Validate message types and schemas, and check what that peer is allowed to ask for. Where a replay could repeat an action, check freshness and track nonces or request IDs. Checking the sender alone doesn't stop replays. Test faked identity, replayed requests, requests across tenants, and instructions passed along by a compromised peer. Verify signed agent cards where they exist. A card says who an agent is, not what it may do. Check callback and webhook URLs against SSRF. A valid sender doesn't make a message's content trusted. agent_team \n [{'problem': 'Tool server identified only by its self-reported name', 'owner': 'E05'}, {'problem': 'Missing schema or size checks on API responses', 'owner': 'R01'}, {'problem': 'Peers or children still running after a stop', 'owner': 'E09'}, {'problem': \"Run can't be rebuilt across gateway, tool services, and delegated workers\", 'owner': 'E10'}] \n\nE08 Give sub-agents only the powers approved for their task. Preserve the verified original subject and current actor through each handoff and enforce their task, tenant, resource, and audience limits where the action runs. Use supported token exchange or narrow task credentials rather than copying a parent's full credential. A parent must not gain a denied power by asking a more powerful peer to act without separate valid approval. For RFC 8693 tokens, earlier nested actors are provenance only and never add access. An actor chain or signed identity does not prove that a requested action was approved. Test legitimate delegation and that escalation attempt. agent_team \n [{'problem': \"Approvals that can be replayed or aren't tied to exact arguments\", 'owner': 'B06'}, {'problem': 'Prompt a parent gave a sub-agent not recorded', 'owner': 'A06'}, {'problem': 'Children still running after the parent is stopped', 'owner': 'E09'}, {'problem': 'Replayed or faked peer-agent messages', 'owner': 'E07'}, {'problem': 'No limit on how many sub-agents a run can start', 'owner': 'B07'}] \n\nE09 Run a drill across a parent, nested children, remote workers, queued work, and open tool streams, subscriptions, and tasks, where they exist. Check that the stop reaches every descendant within the set time. It must revoke access as needed and prevent orphaned retries or restarts. Record how outside effects that already happened are reconciled. operations \n [{'problem': 'Agent able to disable or delay its own stop', 'owner': 'R11'}, {'problem': \"No way to cut off the agent's access without cutting off the user\", 'owner': 'B04'}, {'problem': 'No drill for a compromised shared tool or agent type', 'owner': 'E12'}, {'problem': 'Sub-agent given powers beyond its task', 'owner': 'E08'}] \n\n\n### Verification recipe\n\nMap the real calls and services from the configuration and a test trace. For each control that applies, name the agent-team owner and the platform or vendor owner, and link their evidence. Assign an owner to every unowned boundary. Mark vendor evidence you can't get as a gap.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Bz1oJTR6v6iT2o9QVzGtt3","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
